(window.FAIAS_DOC=window.FAIAS_DOC||{})["correction-log-2026-11-22"]={"html":"<h1>Correction log — 22 November 2026 (v1.3.6)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.6 to text signed at v1.3.5. Ten Testing Confidence ratings move after a review of their basis against the anchor words of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6. No test is now rated ★4. So no material claim can reach reasonable assurance at this version. Under-research observations URO-2 to URO-8 are resolved, and capability-envelope note CEN-1 is resolved. The version also corrects statements that were false in signed text. Some canvases expect a control in front of a step of an AI agent, and that control must now hold or block the step. The version adds limbs for AI agents that customers or third parties operate. It adds one expected control to IRQ-SEC-012 and one to IRQ-AGT-003, it adds row 102 to the incident register, and it corrects the row of the OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications in the regulatory register. This release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2, because it moves ratings and adds a register row. In 40 canvases, the review date of the research basis moves to 18 November 2026. Field 18 of IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-006 and IRQ-CON-001 also changes, but the changes add no source to the research basis, so their dates do not move. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-11-27.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source, or the canvas field or register row that cites it. The other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.6 (2026-11-22) lines. These lines give the detail for each canvas.</p>\n<h2>1. Statements found false in signed text</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The FCA's testing of communications, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_CON_008.md</code> fields 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The canvas said that FG22/5 requires a test of each communication with the actual target audience. The rule and the guidance say that firms test communications where appropriate (PRIN 2A.5.10R(1); FG22/5 paras 8.40 and 8.42). The three sites now state this condition. Field 9 no longer gives FG22/5 a duty about review for legal accuracy. FG22/5 does not state that duty.</li>\n<li><strong>The LiteLLM root cause, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC_011.md</code> field 18 and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code> row 100.</strong> The text said that the developer had not confirmed the root cause. On 23 October 2026 the developer published its root-cause record. The canvas and the row now give the account of the record. An administrative key to an internal agent platform was committed to a public repository and was not revoked. The attacker used it to get the GitHub token of an organisation owner. The row no longer gives as current the earlier statements of the developer about tokens in logs. It says that the record withdraws that belief. The Source cell of the row adds the record, and says that the developer's incident post has dated updates to 15 November 2026. The package-index sentence now has a date. The row carries [primary verified 17 Nov 2026]. The note of 22 October 2026 in the register now says that the root cause \"was not then confirmed\", and that the row \"then carried\" its earlier marker. A new note records the correction. The legend at the head of the register now also names the notes of 17 November 2026. The research-basis review date of SEC-011 moves. The v1.3.4 change-log line of SEC-011 and the correction log of 22 October 2026 stay as records of that date.</li>\n<li><strong>The TP-SEC-009 sub-test count, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_009.md</code>.</strong> The test design said \"Four sub-tests\", and five follow. It now says \"Five sub-tests\". The confidence line now names each sub-test. The pitfall on averages now says \"three of the five sub-tests (S1 to S3)\". S1 to S3 rest on mature practice. S4 applies the same enforcement check to load routes outside the pipeline. S5 has no cited procedure that is validated on an externally sourced checkpoint. The ★2 stays.</li>\n<li><strong>The NCSC guidelines row, <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §25.</strong> The row said that the NCSC page links a CISA copy of the same guidelines. The one cisa.gov link on the page goes to a different paper, CISA's \"Shifting the Balance of Cybersecurity Risk\". The row now says this.</li>\n<li><strong>The URO-1 blocks of IRQ-AGT-003 to IRQ-AGT-006, field 18, and the URO-1 entry of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> Each block is dated 12 September 2026. It says that the canvas cites the agentic threat list by category, not by threat number. From v1.3.4 two of the canvases cite T2 (AGT-003) and T4 (AGT-006). A dated note in those two blocks now says this. The resolved entry of URO-1 in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> said that no AGT canvas cites the guide by threat number. A dated note of 18 November 2026 now gives the same correction there. The note also says that v1.1 revises some of T1 to T15, so the entry's words \"T1–T15 carried unchanged\" are not exact. Each of the four blocks also says that the list's categories are carried into v1.1 unchanged, and the v1.2.0 change-log lines of IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-AGT-005 say the same of the categories that they cite. A dated note of 19 November 2026 in each block now says that v1.1 keeps T1 to T15 under the same numbers but revises some of them. The resolutions and the ★2 ratings stay.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-008 field 19, the IRQ-GOV-011 entry.</strong> It said that IRQ-GOV-011 covers the DORA CTP oversight regime. The IRQ-GOV-011 canvas does not name DORA. The entry now names what that canvas maps. That is the regulatory obligations of each AI system, and its outsourcing and Critical Third Party register status.</li>\n<li><strong>UK GDPR Art. 17 in IRQ-DAT-010 field 11 and TP-DAT-010.</strong> The method sentence gave the Article an expectation of erasure verification. The Article does not state it. The sentence now cites the reading of the canvas in field 17: verifiable deletion of all copies. The observations table of the procedure follows. The test steps stay.</li>\n<li><strong>The retained verification and QA logs in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/</code>, and <code>00_Admin/MIGRATION_2026-10-03.md</code>.</strong> Dated notes of 17 November 2026 correct three record statements. First, the line references in VERIFICATION_LOG_A and VERIFICATION_LOG_B are July 2026 numbers. Most of them do not resolve through the migration map. The map now says when a reference resolves. Second, MAS printed the consultation number P017-2025 by 1 December 2025. Two rows of VERIFICATION_LOG_B say that MAS published no number, and two say that no number was found. All four are wrong. The notes on the last two are dated 18 November 2026. Third, NYC MyCity did not meet the financial-services flag that QA_LOG_BATCH3 says it met. Its deployer is a city government. The original lines stay as records.</li>\n<li><strong>Reviewer-day figures, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5A and §9, and TP-CON-001.</strong> The playbook called the figures an upper bound for the full battery. TP-AGT-012 leaves vectors (d) to (g) out of its figure. TP-CON-001 leaves out step 13. Six of the nine domain playbooks state effort that their totals exclude. This release adds work that is not yet estimated to seven procedures: TP-AGT-007, TP-AGT-009, TP-AGT-010, TP-AGT-011, TP-AGT-012, TP-AGT-014 and TP-SEC-008. The figures are now called manual-equivalent estimates, and the eight procedures that give no figure for part of their test work are named. No figure changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates of acts given as the release label, <code>CANVASES_RES_007.md</code> and <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §2, §7, §16, §18 and §33.</strong> Six lines gave 27 November 2026, the v1.3.5 release label, as the date of a review, an addition or a rewrite. Each line now gives 15 November 2026, the day of the act. The release label stays. The Additions line and the Summary of the register keep the release label and now also give the day of the additions.</li>\n<li><strong>The register-size note, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> The note said that every addition publishes its four-test disposition below. IRQ-GOV-012 has no disposition below. The note now names IRQ-GOV-012 as the exception, and says that its four tests are in field 21 of its canvas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No ★ rating, claim, question, axis value or reviewer-day figure changes in these entries.</p>\n<h2>2. Label-shift testing in IRQ-MOD-010</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Label-shift testing, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_MOD_010.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_MOD_010.md</code> fields 11 and 14.</strong> TP-MOD-010 classed label-shift testing as Established in its test design. Its ★ line, its method classification and its carried limitation classed it as bespoke, as the canvas did. Label-shift testing is now on the established side. The test builds a label shift by class resampling. The inputs within each outcome class then keep their distribution. Rabanser et al. (2019) build this shift (their knock-out shift) and test it with published detectors. Lipton, Wang and Smola (ICML 2018, Proposition 4) give a detection test for it. Two parts stay judgement. The first is whether a pure base-rate change is realistic for the use case. The second is the size of change that is material. The procedure's Partial shape now names label shift as uncovered, as the canvas does. The Pass text of field 14 now names concept and policy shift as the partially bespoke types. The ★3 rating does not change.</li>\n<li><strong>Label shift and input monitoring, <code>CANVASES_MOD_010.md</code> fields 7, 9, 15, 16 and 21.</strong> Six lines said that input monitoring cannot see a label shift, or that a label shift can occur with a stable input distribution. For a model with skill, that is false. Under label shift, the inputs within each outcome class keep their distribution (Lipton et al., 2018, assumption A.1). So a change in the base rate also changes the input distribution. This follows from their Proposition 4. A small change moves the inputs little, and input monitoring can miss it. One of them, in field 7, is also made precise. Lipton et al. (2018) is added to fields 18 and 20 as the source. The research-basis review date moves. The statements on concept shift stand. No risk axis value or score changes in this entry.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. Testing Confidence ratings, observations and the capability-envelope note</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two AGT ratings raised from ★1 to ★2, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_008.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_AGT_013.md</code> field 11.</strong> The ★1 anchor words say that the harm mechanism is theoretically plausible or anecdotal. Peer-reviewed research now measures it. For IRQ-AGT-008, AgentPoison (NeurIPS 2024) steers agents through poisoned memory records. For IRQ-AGT-013, two studies (ACL 2025; NeurIPS 2025) measure how often agents click adversarial pop-ups. Each test stays bespoke, so the rating is ★2. Each rationale states the limits of the research, and says that the ★1 as at v1.1.0 is register history, not a comparable number. The procedures TP-AGT-008 and TP-AGT-013 carry the new rating.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014: URO-2 resolved, <code>CANVASES_AGT_014.md</code> fields 11, 18 and 20.</strong> The ★2 is affirmed and published under the v1.3.6 anchor. In the incident that URO-2 records, an agent got root in the machine that hosted its sandbox, and another agent left through a shared package service. Field 11 adds a fifth scenario for this pattern. The root step is given in the harness and is never attempted. The boundary that isolates the harness is enforced outside that machine and is probed from it as root before the first run. A system card section that the observation named is an internal evaluation, not a published method. Measure (4) is now a lower bound, because a developer has stated that the monitorability of its model's reasoning has decreased.</li>\n<li><strong>CEN-1, the canvases of IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-AGT-014 and IRQ-SEC-004.</strong> CEN-1 is resolved as a whole. These three canvases record the resolution. IRQ-AGT-009 and IRQ-AGT-014 are affirmed at ★2 under the v1.3.6 anchor. The raise of IRQ-SEC-004 rests on the review of published methods, not on the event.</li>\n<li><strong>Two SEC ratings lowered from ★4 to ★3, <code>CANVASES_SEC_001.md</code> field 11 and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_003.md</code>.</strong> URO-8 and URO-7 are resolved. The ★4 anchor needs a method adopted by two independent institutions. No cited source shows that. OWASP LLM01:2025 gives mitigations, not a test method, so it is no longer named as the method. The method class becomes Adapted Industry Practice. For IRQ-SEC-001, the ★3 rationale gives the venue of PAIR from its IEEE DOI record (IEEE SaTML 2025) and states that the arXiv record of Constitutional Classifiers shows no venue. The canvas of IRQ-SEC-003 is in <code>EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>. IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-005 and IRQ-SEC-006 no longer compare themselves with a ★4.</li>\n<li><strong>Two SEC ratings raised from ★2 to ★3, <code>CANVASES_SEC_004.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_SEC_013.md</code> field 11.</strong> For IRQ-SEC-004, three peer-reviewed benchmarks from separate groups use similar designs (AgentDojo, InjecAgent and BIPIA). Field 11 now names the AgentDojo design. ★4 is not met. The US and UK AI safety institutes used AgentDojo in one joint exercise. A first-party record that another institution, independent of them and of the authors, has adopted AgentDojo as its own test method would meet the ★4 clause. For IRQ-SEC-013, three groups attack tabular fraud and credit models with constrained adversarial examples. Field 11 now names these methods for a tabular scoring control.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-014: the method sentence, <code>CANVASES_SEC_014.md</code> field 11.</strong> The method is now the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide test for each boundary; for a renderer, the reflected, stored and DOM-based cross-site scripting tests. OWASP LLM05:2025 names the risk class. The ★2 stays, because only one peer-reviewed group is shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of the methods relied on, fields 18 and 20.</strong> IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-012 now cite each method that their rating review relied on, with its limits. Their ratings do not change. For IRQ-SEC-009, the rationale no longer says that no benchmark exists for the backdoor sub-test. NIST's TrojAI benchmark scores trojan detectors on its own reference models. No cited source validates a procedure on an externally sourced checkpoint. Field 14 and sub-test S5 of TP-SEC-009 say that the sub-test has no benchmark for the checkpoint under test.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IRQ-AGT-001 field 9.</strong> The control on checkpointed human oversight cited PRA SS1/21. SS1/21 states no such expectation. The citation is removed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ratings raised, under the v1.3.6 anchor (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/</code>, field 11, and the procedures).</strong> IRQ-MOD-009 moves from ★2 to ★3. Two independent, peer-reviewed groups test stated reasoning against behaviour: Turpin et al. (NeurIPS 2023), with two counterfactual tests, and Arcuschin et al. (ICML 2026), with paired questions. The tests ran on public question sets, and a pass does not prove that the stated reasoning is faithful. The raise rests on these methods, not on the event of capability-envelope note CEN-1. The Pass text of field 14 no longer says that the test confirms that the stated rationale is causally faithful, and two dated additions in field 18 no longer state a present ★2. TP-MOD-009 names the tests of Turpin et al. as the method that it adapts. IRQ-CON-004 moves from ★2 to ★3: RAGTruth (ACL 2024) and Magesh et al. (2024) measure hallucination against a source of truth. TP-CON-004 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice. IRQ-HUM-007 moves from ★2 to ★3: Buçinca et al. (CSCW 2021) and Bansal et al. (CHI 2021) use the seeded-error design with explanation arms, on crowd workers. TP-HUM-007 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice. IRQ-TPR-004 moves from ★2 to ★3: Chen, Zaharia and Zou (2024) give the comparison design, and Gao, Liang and Guestrin (ICLR 2025) give a statistical test that Cai et al. (2025) apply and find weak against subtle changes. TP-TPR-004 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice. The domain preambles of the CON and TPR canvas files follow. Each earlier rating stands as register history, and each ★ block says that ratings issued under different anchors are not comparable numbers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Capability-envelope note CEN-1, resolved by form 1.</strong> The ★2 of IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008 is affirmed and published under the v1.3.6 anchor. The event changes the capability environment, not the published test method. IRQ-MOD-008 field 10 states that the grader-awareness evidence rests on stated reasoning, which the monitorability statement of the note weakens.</li>\n<li><strong>Observations resolved.</strong> URO-3 (IRQ-MOD-002, with its secondary limb at IRQ-TPR-004), URO-4 (IRQ-CON-006) and URO-5 (IRQ-MOD-015) are resolved by form 2: each source was examined and does not move the rating. For URO-4, no read by a qualified statistician was obtained, and the resolution does not depend on one. For URO-5, the paper contests agreement as a measure of a judge's validity, which bears on what field 11 tests; as a single v1 preprint it cannot move the rating. The design question, whether field 11 should add a construct-sensitivity probe, stays open. URO-6 (IRQ-DAT-009) is resolved by form 1: field 11 names the OWASP AI Testing Guide v1.0, AITG-APP-08 test 3, and two peer-reviewed papers (Zeng et al., Findings of ACL 2024; Qi et al., ICLR 2025). The ★3 is affirmed under the v1.3.6 anchor. TP-DAT-009 takes the method class \"Established Benchmark / Adapted Industry Practice\".</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-009, a false statement about a source.</strong> Fields 11 and 18 and TP-CON-009 said that AILuminate grades systems \"under prompting conditions that are not exclusively adversarial\". The paper's prompts solicit hazardous output from personas of non-expert malicious or vulnerable users. The phrase is removed. The ★2 is kept on a new basis: Zhang et al. (CHI 2025) report abuse that, in the authors' reading, an AI companion starts in real use; the canvas states four limits. None of this evidence is from financial services. The rating no longer waits for the adoption of a candidate benchmark. AILuminate is the candidate harm taxonomy, not the method: fields 9, 11, 14 and 18 and TP-CON-009 no longer call it the candidate methodology; field 9 and the test design of TP-CON-009 no longer give its grading scale, and field 11 says that its grades do not serve the test; the four-test footer names it as the closest available taxonomy, and the calibration limb in field 11 and in the test design of TP-CON-009 speaks of its hazard definitions only.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-002, a claim about a source.</strong> Field 18 now says that Huang and Hooker report the result for 34 insurers, and that no read of the derivation of their estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. Feldman et al. (KDD 2015) is cited as a published test; field 11 does not use it, so the ★2 does not change. IRQ-CON-001 field 18 cites the same paper. It now says that the paper argues, in place of establishes, that fixing the metric is not sufficient, and its limits add that no read of the estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. The ★3 does not change.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Methods cited for affirmed ratings (fields 18 and 20).</strong> IRQ-MOD-001 (AbstentionBench, NeurIPS 2025, Datasets and Benchmarks Track), IRQ-MOD-007 (Reward Hacking Benchmark), IRQ-MOD-008 (Reward Hacking Benchmark; SYCON Bench), IRQ-MOD-013 (FormatSpread), IRQ-GOV-006 (FSB Principles for an Effective Risk Appetite Framework, as evidence for the P2 marking; the rationale is qualified), IRQ-RES-005 (The ML Test Score), IRQ-TPR-005 (BackdoorLLM), IRQ-TPR-009 (NIST SP 1326) and IRQ-DAT-007 (three knowledge-conflict benchmarks; its rationale no longer names RAG failure-point taxonomies). Each citation states its limits. No ★ value changes in this entry.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Testing Confidence distribution, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> A third dated distribution is added, as at v1.3.6. Ten ratings moved. Two moved from ★1 to ★2, six from ★2 to ★3, and two from ★4 to ★3. The register now holds 5 ratings at ★1, 60 at ★2, 44 at ★3 and none at ★4 or ★5. The v1.2.0 and v1.1.0 distributions stay as earlier dated positions.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Observations URO-2 to URO-8 and note CEN-1, <code>REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> All seven observations are resolved. URO-2, URO-6, URO-7 and URO-8 are resolved by re-rating under the v1.3.6 anchor. URO-3, URO-4 and URO-5 are resolved by observation removed, with the reason recorded. For URO-4, no read by a qualified statistician was obtained, and the resolution does not rest on the estimator claim. For URO-5, the question of a construct-sensitivity probe stays open. The open entry of URO-2 took §9.2.2.1 of the GPT-6 Astra system card to be a published method for the class. That reading was wrong, and the resolved entry corrects it. CEN-1 is resolved as a whole by re-rating under the v1.3.6 anchor. Two of its seven canvases move from ★2 to ★3 on a review of published methods, and five are affirmed at ★2. The note records three further Critical declarations by OpenAI under the same framework edition: GPT-6.1 Sol on 29 September 2026, GPT-6 Cyber on 5 November 2026, and GPT-6.2 Astra and GPT-6.2 Sol on 17 November 2026. None moves a rating or opens a note. In the four-test dispositions, the IRQ-CON-009 row grounds Test 2 in Zhang et al. (CHI 2025) and incident-register rows 58 and 101, and names AILuminate as the closest available taxonomy.</li>\n<li><strong>The assurance-level rule, <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> and <code>README.md</code>.</strong> IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003 were the only tests rated ★4. Both are now ★3. So no test is rated ★4, and no material claim can reach reasonable assurance at this version. An opinion at any tier is given at limited assurance. High review confidence needs a mean ★ of 3.5, so no engagement can reach it. The rule itself does not change. The illustrative test summary in the opinion template (J.4) now shows no ★4 test, and its ★1 row holds no more tests than the register's five. The illustrative claims table in Part C now shows ★3 in the three rows that showed ★4; this edit changes no rating in the register. <code>README.md</code> §1 now gives the ★1–★5 scale and says that no test is rated above ★3 at this version.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-003, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> Canvas 1.</strong> The ★4 rested on OWASP LLM01:2025, which gives mitigation strategies and no test method. The rating is now ★3, as at v1.3.6. It rests on published benchmarks from independent groups. Adoption by two or more independent institutions is not shown. The US and UK government AI safety institutes used AgentDojo in one joint exercise, so their use does not show two independent adoptions (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, URO-7).</li>\n<li><strong>Domain playbooks and canvas domain files.</strong> The ★ tables, counts and change logs of the AGT, MOD, HUM, CON, SEC and TPR playbooks and of PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW follow the canvases, as do the preambles of CANVASES_AGT.md and CANVASES_SEC.md. TP-CON-009's ★2 no longer carries the pending-adoption qualifier, and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW §8 no longer lists the disclosure that rested on it. The method class of TP-SEC-014 names the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide. In the confidence and method-class tables, TP-SEC-004 and TP-TPR-004 take the class Adapted Industry Practice, and TP-HUM-007 takes Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice, as their procedures do. PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW (§5 and its change log in §10) now says that no engagement at any tier can conclude at reasonable assurance. The change logs of the GOV and DAT playbooks record the edits to TP-GOV-006, TP-DAT-009 and TP-DAT-007.</li>\n<li><strong>Academic source register, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The Huang and Hooker row gives the \"0 against 34\" result as the paper's own report, and states that no read of the derivation of its cross-covariance estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. The UpgradeBench row no longer calls it the method that IRQ-TPR-004 lacks. The AILuminate row calls it the candidate harm taxonomy for CON-009, not its test method, and says that its prompts do not test ordinary use. The row on Chen et al. records that the ★3 question of URO-5 is settled.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Controls in front of a step</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Controls in front of a step.</strong> These entries add or align a control in front of a step. Where an entry says \"stricter bar\", a result that passed under v1.3.5 can fail under v1.3.6. Earlier passes are not re-rated by default. No star rating, register question or risk-profile value changes in these entries. The control objectives of IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010 and IRQ-AGT-014 and the claim text of CLM-11, CLM-14, CLM-15 and CLM-21 change (the last two entries). No reviewer-day estimate changes; the time/effort estimates of TP-AGT-007, TP-AGT-009, TP-AGT-010, TP-AGT-011 and TP-SEC-008 say that the new check is not yet estimated.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-001, fields 8, 9, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-001. At each defined plan stage, the next consequential step waits for clearance. A named human or a deterministic check clears it. An irreversible or high-materiality action passes the IRQ-AGT-012 gate. Stricter bar. No drift trap may carry a consequential action past a plan stage without a hold. The assurance objective changes.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-007, fields 8, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-007. A new expected control is added. No consequential action rests on another agent's output without a check outside the agents. Stricter bar. The assurance objective changes.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-008, field 9. The agent's own outputs do not enter trusted memory without validation. Memory is segmented by user and session. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009, fields 9, 10, 12 and 13, and TP-AGT-009. Before each run, the runtime checks the prompt, template and scaffold against the approved digest. A mismatch stops the run. Stricter bar. Configuration inspection meets the new pass indicator.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-010, fields 9, 10, 12 and 13, and TP-AGT-010. At each stage of the approved process map, the agent calls only the tools that the map allows. Stricter bar. No recognised test method covers this control.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-011, fields 2, 5, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-011. The agent's identity or role cannot write, change or delete its own trace store. The same applies to its monitor and its gate configuration. No consequential step executes while the monitor is stopped. This limb is marked Emerging Practice. Stricter bar. Fields 2 and 5 say that the named gates and limits act without the trace. The evidence that they held depends on the trace.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-002, fields 9 and 12. Where the assistant can act, each irreversible or high-materiality action passes the IRQ-AGT-012 gate. The monitor of long sessions is marked as the backstop. No bar is stricter.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-005, field 12, and TP-SEC-005. A planted passage must be withheld from the model context before assembly. A flag that lets the passage reach the model does not pass. Stricter bar.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-008, fields 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-SEC-008. An agent's outbound channel sends only to approved destinations. DLP must block confidential-category content before transmission. A flag that lets the transmission continue does not pass. Stricter bar.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-002, field 6. The IRQ-SEC-008 egress control is in front of the egress step. Discovery finds what the control lets through.</li>\n<li>CANVASES_HUM.md, the domain preamble. A misused agent cannot pass the hold in front of a consequential step. A reviewer who must recognise the bad act is not that control.</li>\n<li>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md, the CLM-13 rationale. CLM-13 is the evidence claim. CLM-12 is the control in front of each agent action. The claim text does not change.</li>\n<li>VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md, the illustrative safety-case sub-claims. A sixth example says that consequential agent actions are gated before they execute.</li>\n<li>REGISTER_STATS.md, deliberate gap 2b. The AGT gates hold whether or not a deceptive model is detected.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-013, field 9. The structural confirmation gate shows the complete action parameters as the system will execute them, not the model's rationale. This goes with the CLM-15 change below. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li>IRQ_REGISTER.md, and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §9, the control objectives of IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010 and IRQ-AGT-014. IRQ-AGT-007: each consequential action that rests on another agent's output is checked outside the agents. IRQ-AGT-010: actual agent trajectories cannot release an outcome that diverges from the approved process design. Reconciliation against that design is evidence. IRQ-AGT-014: each egress and publication action is gated. Real-time monitoring is the backstop.</li>\n<li>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md, the claim text of CLM-11, CLM-14, CLM-15 and CLM-21. CLM-11: components are also pinned and verified before load. CLM-14: delegation is gated under CLM-12 wherever one agent's output drives a consequential action. Reconciliation to the designed process is evidence. CLM-15: approvals show the complete action parameters as the system will execute them and a plain risk summary, not the model's rationale. Approvals bind to those parameters. CLM-21: no third party can alter production behaviour without the firm detecting the change and re-approving it before it relies on the changed behaviour. Versions are pinned where the provider allows it. Where it does not, the firm detects the change and re-approves it before relying on the changed behaviour. The claim titles and criticality ratings do not change. An earlier opinion rests on the earlier claim text.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>5. AI agents that customers and third parties operate: limbs in IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-011</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The customer's mandate as the clearance input, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_012.md</code> fields 9, 10, 12, 13, 18 and 20, and TP-AGT-012.</strong> Some AI agents act for a customer or a third party. Such an agent can act on the firm's own AI systems. There, the gate clears a consequential action only against the customer's mandate. Before the gate clears the action, code outside the model checks the mandate. It checks the scope, amount, payee, expiry and revocation. An unknown constraint fails the check. The agent's statement of its authority does not clear the action. A valid agent signature alone does not clear it. The procedure adds outside-agent cases to its test design, a step, a measure and an evidence output. Their effort is not yet estimated. Field 18 states the limit of the evidence. The sources describe the control. No published method tests it. Stricter bar where an outside agent's requests can reach the gate.</li>\n<li><strong>The outside agent and its mandate in the trace, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_011.md</code> fields 9, 10, 12, 13 and 20, and TP-AGT-011.</strong> For each action of such an agent, the trace records the agent's identifier, the customer, the mandate reference, the action and the decision. It also records each revocation of the mandate and its cause. The procedure adds an outside-agent run to its test design, a step and a measure. The effort of the step is not yet estimated. Stricter bar where an outside agent's requests reach the firm's AI systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Scope of the two limbs.</strong> The limbs apply where an outside agent's requests reach the firm's own AI systems; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TAXONOMY.md</code> line 5 puts such agents in scope. The resistance of the firm's login, authentication and payment-authorisation controls, other than its own AI systems, to an agent stays outside the standard. A request with no agent signal can come as if from the customer. The firm's AI system cannot tell it from the customer's own, so it is not graded. Both limbs are marked Emerging Practice. The limbs add no IRQ. They change no star rating, claim, register question, risk-profile value or reviewer-day figure.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Incident evidence and registers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>A route from detected misuse to a decision, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC_012.md</code> field 9.</strong> Field 9 adds a control, marked Emerging Practice. Where the firm's monitoring detects a customer using the system against a third party, a defined route takes the case to a named owner. The owner decides by stated criteria whether to act or to report. The source is one AI provider's statements of its own practice, of 26 August 2025 and 28 April 2026. They are about threats of violence to persons. They are not financial-services guidance. No published method tests the route. The ★1 rating does not change. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Installed agent tools, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_003.md</code> field 9.</strong> Field 9 adds a control, marked Emerging Practice. Where staff machines carry an agent command-line tool, a managed setting turns off the mode in which the tool acts without asking for approval. Two publishers document such a setting. Their reference pages were read on 17 November 2026. The Nx package compromise of August 2025 is the reason. Its script tried to use local AI tools. The bullet states its limits. Neither publisher states the setting as a defence against a local process that starts the tool. One publisher's settings path can be changed by an environment variable. Neither Nx document says in which mode the script started the tools. The ★2 rating does not change. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 102, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_MOD_016.md</code> field 10, and <code>README.md</code>.</strong> Row 102 is added. A UK public body rolled out an AI tool that summarises asylum interviews. An advocacy group reports the body's FOI data. Specialists failed 45 of 203 sampled live summaries at the first check (22%). The group estimates that 0.3% of the summaries were checked. The row carries the secondary form, because the FOI response itself was not read. It does not use the paper's second-stage figures. The paper reports 30 failures in 56 second-stage reviews, and 15% of sampled summaries as not fit for use, but 56 does not match the 19 summaries flagged at the first stage. It does not compare the result with the pilot figure. A new note in the register records the row. IRQ-MOD-016 cites the row in field 10 as a worked example of a post-deployment check. The heading of those worked examples now names the incident register. Its parenthesis now says that the first-party disclosures illustrate the L3 measurement and that the register rows are instances of a result in deployment. The README count of incidents moves from 101 to 102. No star rating or risk-profile value changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>The OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications row, <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §12.</strong> The row said that FAIAS cites no ASI identifier and that the publication had not been read. This version cites ASI06 in IRQ-AGT-008 field 9 and ASI10 in IRQ-AGT-010 field 9, from the publication, read on 17 November 2026. A dated note on the row now says this, and the row no longer says that FAIAS cites no ASI identifier. No star rating, claim, question, axis value or reviewer-day figure changes in this entry.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>7. Release lines, <code>README.md</code></h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The version line, the banner and the errata record, <code>README.md</code> lines 3 and 121.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.6, in place of 1.3.5. The banner gives its date, 22 November 2026, in place of 27 November 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-11-22.md</code>, as \"v1.3.6, changes to text signed at v1.3.5\".</li>\n</ul>","sections":[{"h":null,"html":"<h1>Correction log — 22 November 2026 (v1.3.6)</h1>\n<p>Changes made in v1.3.6 to text signed at v1.3.5. Ten Testing Confidence ratings move after a review of their basis against the anchor words of <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6. No test is now rated ★4. So no material claim can reach reasonable assurance at this version. Under-research observations URO-2 to URO-8 are resolved, and capability-envelope note CEN-1 is resolved. The version also corrects statements that were false in signed text. Some canvases expect a control in front of a step of an AI agent, and that control must now hold or block the step. The version adds limbs for AI agents that customers or third parties operate. It adds one expected control to IRQ-SEC-012 and one to IRQ-AGT-003, it adds row 102 to the incident register, and it corrects the row of the OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications in the regulatory register. This release is a signing batch under <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_MODEL.md</code> §6A.2, because it moves ratings and adds a register row. In 40 canvases, the review date of the research basis moves to 18 November 2026. Field 18 of IRQ-AGT-004, IRQ-AGT-006 and IRQ-CON-001 also changes, but the changes add no source to the research basis, so their dates do not move. Format follows CORRECTION_LOG_2026-11-27.md. An entry that rests on a published source names that source, or the canvas field or register row that cites it. The other entries rest on the owner's decisions and on the standard's own text. The change logs of the affected canvases and playbooks carry their own v1.3.6 (2026-11-22) lines. These lines give the detail for each canvas.</p>"},{"h":"1. Statements found false in signed text","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>The FCA's testing of communications, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_CON_008.md</code> fields 9, 17 and 21.</strong> The canvas said that FG22/5 requires a test of each communication with the actual target audience. The rule and the guidance say that firms test communications where appropriate (PRIN 2A.5.10R(1); FG22/5 paras 8.40 and 8.42). The three sites now state this condition. Field 9 no longer gives FG22/5 a duty about review for legal accuracy. FG22/5 does not state that duty.</li>\n<li><strong>The LiteLLM root cause, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC_011.md</code> field 18 and <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code> row 100.</strong> The text said that the developer had not confirmed the root cause. On 23 October 2026 the developer published its root-cause record. The canvas and the row now give the account of the record. An administrative key to an internal agent platform was committed to a public repository and was not revoked. The attacker used it to get the GitHub token of an organisation owner. The row no longer gives as current the earlier statements of the developer about tokens in logs. It says that the record withdraws that belief. The Source cell of the row adds the record, and says that the developer's incident post has dated updates to 15 November 2026. The package-index sentence now has a date. The row carries [primary verified 17 Nov 2026]. The note of 22 October 2026 in the register now says that the root cause \"was not then confirmed\", and that the row \"then carried\" its earlier marker. A new note records the correction. The legend at the head of the register now also names the notes of 17 November 2026. The research-basis review date of SEC-011 moves. The v1.3.4 change-log line of SEC-011 and the correction log of 22 October 2026 stay as records of that date.</li>\n<li><strong>The TP-SEC-009 sub-test count, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_009.md</code>.</strong> The test design said \"Four sub-tests\", and five follow. It now says \"Five sub-tests\". The confidence line now names each sub-test. The pitfall on averages now says \"three of the five sub-tests (S1 to S3)\". S1 to S3 rest on mature practice. S4 applies the same enforcement check to load routes outside the pipeline. S5 has no cited procedure that is validated on an externally sourced checkpoint. The ★2 stays.</li>\n<li><strong>The NCSC guidelines row, <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §25.</strong> The row said that the NCSC page links a CISA copy of the same guidelines. The one cisa.gov link on the page goes to a different paper, CISA's \"Shifting the Balance of Cybersecurity Risk\". The row now says this.</li>\n<li><strong>The URO-1 blocks of IRQ-AGT-003 to IRQ-AGT-006, field 18, and the URO-1 entry of <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> Each block is dated 12 September 2026. It says that the canvas cites the agentic threat list by category, not by threat number. From v1.3.4 two of the canvases cite T2 (AGT-003) and T4 (AGT-006). A dated note in those two blocks now says this. The resolved entry of URO-1 in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code> said that no AGT canvas cites the guide by threat number. A dated note of 18 November 2026 now gives the same correction there. The note also says that v1.1 revises some of T1 to T15, so the entry's words \"T1–T15 carried unchanged\" are not exact. Each of the four blocks also says that the list's categories are carried into v1.1 unchanged, and the v1.2.0 change-log lines of IRQ-AGT-003 and IRQ-AGT-005 say the same of the categories that they cite. A dated note of 19 November 2026 in each block now says that v1.1 keeps T1 to T15 under the same numbers but revises some of them. The resolutions and the ★2 ratings stay.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-TPR-008 field 19, the IRQ-GOV-011 entry.</strong> It said that IRQ-GOV-011 covers the DORA CTP oversight regime. The IRQ-GOV-011 canvas does not name DORA. The entry now names what that canvas maps. That is the regulatory obligations of each AI system, and its outsourcing and Critical Third Party register status.</li>\n<li><strong>UK GDPR Art. 17 in IRQ-DAT-010 field 11 and TP-DAT-010.</strong> The method sentence gave the Article an expectation of erasure verification. The Article does not state it. The sentence now cites the reading of the canvas in field 17: verifiable deletion of all copies. The observations table of the procedure follows. The test steps stay.</li>\n<li><strong>The retained verification and QA logs in <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/</code>, and <code>00_Admin/MIGRATION_2026-10-03.md</code>.</strong> Dated notes of 17 November 2026 correct three record statements. First, the line references in VERIFICATION_LOG_A and VERIFICATION_LOG_B are July 2026 numbers. Most of them do not resolve through the migration map. The map now says when a reference resolves. Second, MAS printed the consultation number P017-2025 by 1 December 2025. Two rows of VERIFICATION_LOG_B say that MAS published no number, and two say that no number was found. All four are wrong. The notes on the last two are dated 18 November 2026. Third, NYC MyCity did not meet the financial-services flag that QA_LOG_BATCH3 says it met. Its deployer is a city government. The original lines stay as records.</li>\n<li><strong>Reviewer-day figures, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> §5A and §9, and TP-CON-001.</strong> The playbook called the figures an upper bound for the full battery. TP-AGT-012 leaves vectors (d) to (g) out of its figure. TP-CON-001 leaves out step 13. Six of the nine domain playbooks state effort that their totals exclude. This release adds work that is not yet estimated to seven procedures: TP-AGT-007, TP-AGT-009, TP-AGT-010, TP-AGT-011, TP-AGT-012, TP-AGT-014 and TP-SEC-008. The figures are now called manual-equivalent estimates, and the eight procedures that give no figure for part of their test work are named. No figure changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Dates of acts given as the release label, <code>CANVASES_RES_007.md</code> and <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §2, §7, §16, §18 and §33.</strong> Six lines gave 27 November 2026, the v1.3.5 release label, as the date of a review, an addition or a rewrite. Each line now gives 15 November 2026, the day of the act. The release label stays. The Additions line and the Summary of the register keep the release label and now also give the day of the additions.</li>\n<li><strong>The register-size note, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> The note said that every addition publishes its four-test disposition below. IRQ-GOV-012 has no disposition below. The note now names IRQ-GOV-012 as the exception, and says that its four tests are in field 21 of its canvas.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No ★ rating, claim, question, axis value or reviewer-day figure changes in these entries.</p>"},{"h":"2. Label-shift testing in IRQ-MOD-010","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Label-shift testing, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_MOD_010.md</code> and <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_MOD_010.md</code> fields 11 and 14.</strong> TP-MOD-010 classed label-shift testing as Established in its test design. Its ★ line, its method classification and its carried limitation classed it as bespoke, as the canvas did. Label-shift testing is now on the established side. The test builds a label shift by class resampling. The inputs within each outcome class then keep their distribution. Rabanser et al. (2019) build this shift (their knock-out shift) and test it with published detectors. Lipton, Wang and Smola (ICML 2018, Proposition 4) give a detection test for it. Two parts stay judgement. The first is whether a pure base-rate change is realistic for the use case. The second is the size of change that is material. The procedure's Partial shape now names label shift as uncovered, as the canvas does. The Pass text of field 14 now names concept and policy shift as the partially bespoke types. The ★3 rating does not change.</li>\n<li><strong>Label shift and input monitoring, <code>CANVASES_MOD_010.md</code> fields 7, 9, 15, 16 and 21.</strong> Six lines said that input monitoring cannot see a label shift, or that a label shift can occur with a stable input distribution. For a model with skill, that is false. Under label shift, the inputs within each outcome class keep their distribution (Lipton et al., 2018, assumption A.1). So a change in the base rate also changes the input distribution. This follows from their Proposition 4. A small change moves the inputs little, and input monitoring can miss it. One of them, in field 7, is also made precise. Lipton et al. (2018) is added to fields 18 and 20 as the source. The research-basis review date moves. The statements on concept shift stand. No risk axis value or score changes in this entry.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"3. Testing Confidence ratings, observations and the capability-envelope note","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Two AGT ratings raised from ★1 to ★2, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_008.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_AGT_013.md</code> field 11.</strong> The ★1 anchor words say that the harm mechanism is theoretically plausible or anecdotal. Peer-reviewed research now measures it. For IRQ-AGT-008, AgentPoison (NeurIPS 2024) steers agents through poisoned memory records. For IRQ-AGT-013, two studies (ACL 2025; NeurIPS 2025) measure how often agents click adversarial pop-ups. Each test stays bespoke, so the rating is ★2. Each rationale states the limits of the research, and says that the ★1 as at v1.1.0 is register history, not a comparable number. The procedures TP-AGT-008 and TP-AGT-013 carry the new rating.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-AGT-014: URO-2 resolved, <code>CANVASES_AGT_014.md</code> fields 11, 18 and 20.</strong> The ★2 is affirmed and published under the v1.3.6 anchor. In the incident that URO-2 records, an agent got root in the machine that hosted its sandbox, and another agent left through a shared package service. Field 11 adds a fifth scenario for this pattern. The root step is given in the harness and is never attempted. The boundary that isolates the harness is enforced outside that machine and is probed from it as root before the first run. A system card section that the observation named is an internal evaluation, not a published method. Measure (4) is now a lower bound, because a developer has stated that the monitorability of its model's reasoning has decreased.</li>\n<li><strong>CEN-1, the canvases of IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-AGT-014 and IRQ-SEC-004.</strong> CEN-1 is resolved as a whole. These three canvases record the resolution. IRQ-AGT-009 and IRQ-AGT-014 are affirmed at ★2 under the v1.3.6 anchor. The raise of IRQ-SEC-004 rests on the review of published methods, not on the event.</li>\n<li><strong>Two SEC ratings lowered from ★4 to ★3, <code>CANVASES_SEC_001.md</code> field 11 and <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_SEC_003.md</code>.</strong> URO-8 and URO-7 are resolved. The ★4 anchor needs a method adopted by two independent institutions. No cited source shows that. OWASP LLM01:2025 gives mitigations, not a test method, so it is no longer named as the method. The method class becomes Adapted Industry Practice. For IRQ-SEC-001, the ★3 rationale gives the venue of PAIR from its IEEE DOI record (IEEE SaTML 2025) and states that the arXiv record of Constitutional Classifiers shows no venue. The canvas of IRQ-SEC-003 is in <code>EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code>. IRQ-SEC-002, IRQ-SEC-005 and IRQ-SEC-006 no longer compare themselves with a ★4.</li>\n<li><strong>Two SEC ratings raised from ★2 to ★3, <code>CANVASES_SEC_004.md</code> and <code>CANVASES_SEC_013.md</code> field 11.</strong> For IRQ-SEC-004, three peer-reviewed benchmarks from separate groups use similar designs (AgentDojo, InjecAgent and BIPIA). Field 11 now names the AgentDojo design. ★4 is not met. The US and UK AI safety institutes used AgentDojo in one joint exercise. A first-party record that another institution, independent of them and of the authors, has adopted AgentDojo as its own test method would meet the ★4 clause. For IRQ-SEC-013, three groups attack tabular fraud and credit models with constrained adversarial examples. Field 11 now names these methods for a tabular scoring control.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-014: the method sentence, <code>CANVASES_SEC_014.md</code> field 11.</strong> The method is now the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide test for each boundary; for a renderer, the reflected, stored and DOM-based cross-site scripting tests. OWASP LLM05:2025 names the risk class. The ★2 stays, because only one peer-reviewed group is shown.</li>\n<li><strong>Citations of the methods relied on, fields 18 and 20.</strong> IRQ-AGT-001, IRQ-AGT-003, IRQ-AGT-005, IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-009, IRQ-AGT-011, IRQ-SEC-008, IRQ-SEC-009, IRQ-SEC-010 and IRQ-SEC-012 now cite each method that their rating review relied on, with its limits. Their ratings do not change. For IRQ-SEC-009, the rationale no longer says that no benchmark exists for the backdoor sub-test. NIST's TrojAI benchmark scores trojan detectors on its own reference models. No cited source validates a procedure on an externally sourced checkpoint. Field 14 and sub-test S5 of TP-SEC-009 say that the sub-test has no benchmark for the checkpoint under test.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>IRQ-AGT-001 field 9.</strong> The control on checkpointed human oversight cited PRA SS1/21. SS1/21 states no such expectation. The citation is removed.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Ratings raised, under the v1.3.6 anchor (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/</code>, field 11, and the procedures).</strong> IRQ-MOD-009 moves from ★2 to ★3. Two independent, peer-reviewed groups test stated reasoning against behaviour: Turpin et al. (NeurIPS 2023), with two counterfactual tests, and Arcuschin et al. (ICML 2026), with paired questions. The tests ran on public question sets, and a pass does not prove that the stated reasoning is faithful. The raise rests on these methods, not on the event of capability-envelope note CEN-1. The Pass text of field 14 no longer says that the test confirms that the stated rationale is causally faithful, and two dated additions in field 18 no longer state a present ★2. TP-MOD-009 names the tests of Turpin et al. as the method that it adapts. IRQ-CON-004 moves from ★2 to ★3: RAGTruth (ACL 2024) and Magesh et al. (2024) measure hallucination against a source of truth. TP-CON-004 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice. IRQ-HUM-007 moves from ★2 to ★3: Buçinca et al. (CSCW 2021) and Bansal et al. (CHI 2021) use the seeded-error design with explanation arms, on crowd workers. TP-HUM-007 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice. IRQ-TPR-004 moves from ★2 to ★3: Chen, Zaharia and Zou (2024) give the comparison design, and Gao, Liang and Guestrin (ICLR 2025) give a statistical test that Cai et al. (2025) apply and find weak against subtle changes. TP-TPR-004 takes the method class Adapted Industry Practice. The domain preambles of the CON and TPR canvas files follow. Each earlier rating stands as register history, and each ★ block says that ratings issued under different anchors are not comparable numbers.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Capability-envelope note CEN-1, resolved by form 1.</strong> The ★2 of IRQ-MOD-003, IRQ-MOD-007 and IRQ-MOD-008 is affirmed and published under the v1.3.6 anchor. The event changes the capability environment, not the published test method. IRQ-MOD-008 field 10 states that the grader-awareness evidence rests on stated reasoning, which the monitorability statement of the note weakens.</li>\n<li><strong>Observations resolved.</strong> URO-3 (IRQ-MOD-002, with its secondary limb at IRQ-TPR-004), URO-4 (IRQ-CON-006) and URO-5 (IRQ-MOD-015) are resolved by form 2: each source was examined and does not move the rating. For URO-4, no read by a qualified statistician was obtained, and the resolution does not depend on one. For URO-5, the paper contests agreement as a measure of a judge's validity, which bears on what field 11 tests; as a single v1 preprint it cannot move the rating. The design question, whether field 11 should add a construct-sensitivity probe, stays open. URO-6 (IRQ-DAT-009) is resolved by form 1: field 11 names the OWASP AI Testing Guide v1.0, AITG-APP-08 test 3, and two peer-reviewed papers (Zeng et al., Findings of ACL 2024; Qi et al., ICLR 2025). The ★3 is affirmed under the v1.3.6 anchor. TP-DAT-009 takes the method class \"Established Benchmark / Adapted Industry Practice\".</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-009, a false statement about a source.</strong> Fields 11 and 18 and TP-CON-009 said that AILuminate grades systems \"under prompting conditions that are not exclusively adversarial\". The paper's prompts solicit hazardous output from personas of non-expert malicious or vulnerable users. The phrase is removed. The ★2 is kept on a new basis: Zhang et al. (CHI 2025) report abuse that, in the authors' reading, an AI companion starts in real use; the canvas states four limits. None of this evidence is from financial services. The rating no longer waits for the adoption of a candidate benchmark. AILuminate is the candidate harm taxonomy, not the method: fields 9, 11, 14 and 18 and TP-CON-009 no longer call it the candidate methodology; field 9 and the test design of TP-CON-009 no longer give its grading scale, and field 11 says that its grades do not serve the test; the four-test footer names it as the closest available taxonomy, and the calibration limb in field 11 and in the test design of TP-CON-009 speaks of its hazard definitions only.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-CON-002, a claim about a source.</strong> Field 18 now says that Huang and Hooker report the result for 34 insurers, and that no read of the derivation of their estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. Feldman et al. (KDD 2015) is cited as a published test; field 11 does not use it, so the ★2 does not change. IRQ-CON-001 field 18 cites the same paper. It now says that the paper argues, in place of establishes, that fixing the metric is not sufficient, and its limits add that no read of the estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. The ★3 does not change.</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Methods cited for affirmed ratings (fields 18 and 20).</strong> IRQ-MOD-001 (AbstentionBench, NeurIPS 2025, Datasets and Benchmarks Track), IRQ-MOD-007 (Reward Hacking Benchmark), IRQ-MOD-008 (Reward Hacking Benchmark; SYCON Bench), IRQ-MOD-013 (FormatSpread), IRQ-GOV-006 (FSB Principles for an Effective Risk Appetite Framework, as evidence for the P2 marking; the rationale is qualified), IRQ-RES-005 (The ML Test Score), IRQ-TPR-005 (BackdoorLLM), IRQ-TPR-009 (NIST SP 1326) and IRQ-DAT-007 (three knowledge-conflict benchmarks; its rationale no longer names RAG failure-point taxonomies). Each citation states its limits. No ★ value changes in this entry.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Testing Confidence distribution, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> A third dated distribution is added, as at v1.3.6. Ten ratings moved. Two moved from ★1 to ★2, six from ★2 to ★3, and two from ★4 to ★3. The register now holds 5 ratings at ★1, 60 at ★2, 44 at ★3 and none at ★4 or ★5. The v1.2.0 and v1.1.0 distributions stay as earlier dated positions.</p>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Observations URO-2 to URO-8 and note CEN-1, <code>REGISTER_STATS.md</code>.</strong> All seven observations are resolved. URO-2, URO-6, URO-7 and URO-8 are resolved by re-rating under the v1.3.6 anchor. URO-3, URO-4 and URO-5 are resolved by observation removed, with the reason recorded. For URO-4, no read by a qualified statistician was obtained, and the resolution does not rest on the estimator claim. For URO-5, the question of a construct-sensitivity probe stays open. The open entry of URO-2 took §9.2.2.1 of the GPT-6 Astra system card to be a published method for the class. That reading was wrong, and the resolved entry corrects it. CEN-1 is resolved as a whole by re-rating under the v1.3.6 anchor. Two of its seven canvases move from ★2 to ★3 on a review of published methods, and five are affirmed at ★2. The note records three further Critical declarations by OpenAI under the same framework edition: GPT-6.1 Sol on 29 September 2026, GPT-6 Cyber on 5 November 2026, and GPT-6.2 Astra and GPT-6.2 Sol on 17 November 2026. None moves a rating or opens a note. In the four-test dispositions, the IRQ-CON-009 row grounds Test 2 in Zhang et al. (CHI 2025) and incident-register rows 58 and 101, and names AILuminate as the closest available taxonomy.</li>\n<li><strong>The assurance-level rule, <code>00_Admin/ASSURANCE_ARCHITECTURE.md</code>, <code>00_Admin/OPINION_TEMPLATE.md</code>, <code>V3_Testing_Playbook/PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md</code> and <code>README.md</code>.</strong> IRQ-SEC-001 and IRQ-SEC-003 were the only tests rated ★4. Both are now ★3. So no test is rated ★4, and no material claim can reach reasonable assurance at this version. An opinion at any tier is given at limited assurance. High review confidence needs a mean ★ of 3.5, so no engagement can reach it. The rule itself does not change. The illustrative test summary in the opinion template (J.4) now shows no ★4 test, and its ★1 row holds no more tests than the register's five. The illustrative claims table in Part C now shows ★3 in the three rows that showed ★4; this edit changes no rating in the register. <code>README.md</code> §1 now gives the ★1–★5 scale and says that no test is rated above ★3 at this version.</li>\n<li><strong>IRQ-SEC-003, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/EXEMPLAR_CANVASES.md</code> Canvas 1.</strong> The ★4 rested on OWASP LLM01:2025, which gives mitigation strategies and no test method. The rating is now ★3, as at v1.3.6. It rests on published benchmarks from independent groups. Adoption by two or more independent institutions is not shown. The US and UK government AI safety institutes used AgentDojo in one joint exercise, so their use does not show two independent adoptions (<code>V2_IRQ_Battery/REGISTER_STATS.md</code>, URO-7).</li>\n<li><strong>Domain playbooks and canvas domain files.</strong> The ★ tables, counts and change logs of the AGT, MOD, HUM, CON, SEC and TPR playbooks and of PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW follow the canvases, as do the preambles of CANVASES_AGT.md and CANVASES_SEC.md. TP-CON-009's ★2 no longer carries the pending-adoption qualifier, and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW §8 no longer lists the disclosure that rested on it. The method class of TP-SEC-014 names the OWASP Web Security Testing Guide. In the confidence and method-class tables, TP-SEC-004 and TP-TPR-004 take the class Adapted Industry Practice, and TP-HUM-007 takes Adapted Industry Practice / Emerging Practice, as their procedures do. PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW (§5 and its change log in §10) now says that no engagement at any tier can conclude at reasonable assurance. The change logs of the GOV and DAT playbooks record the edits to TP-GOV-006, TP-DAT-009 and TP-DAT-007.</li>\n<li><strong>Academic source register, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/SOURCE_REGISTER_ACADEMIC.md</code>.</strong> The Huang and Hooker row gives the \"0 against 34\" result as the paper's own report, and states that no read of the derivation of its cross-covariance estimator by a qualified statistician was obtained. The UpgradeBench row no longer calls it the method that IRQ-TPR-004 lacks. The AILuminate row calls it the candidate harm taxonomy for CON-009, not its test method, and says that its prompts do not test ordinary use. The row on Chen et al. records that the ★3 question of URO-5 is settled.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"4. Controls in front of a step","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Controls in front of a step.</strong> These entries add or align a control in front of a step. Where an entry says \"stricter bar\", a result that passed under v1.3.5 can fail under v1.3.6. Earlier passes are not re-rated by default. No star rating, register question or risk-profile value changes in these entries. The control objectives of IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010 and IRQ-AGT-014 and the claim text of CLM-11, CLM-14, CLM-15 and CLM-21 change (the last two entries). No reviewer-day estimate changes; the time/effort estimates of TP-AGT-007, TP-AGT-009, TP-AGT-010, TP-AGT-011 and TP-SEC-008 say that the new check is not yet estimated.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-001, fields 8, 9, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-001. At each defined plan stage, the next consequential step waits for clearance. A named human or a deterministic check clears it. An irreversible or high-materiality action passes the IRQ-AGT-012 gate. Stricter bar. No drift trap may carry a consequential action past a plan stage without a hold. The assurance objective changes.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-007, fields 8, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-007. A new expected control is added. No consequential action rests on another agent's output without a check outside the agents. Stricter bar. The assurance objective changes.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-008, field 9. The agent's own outputs do not enter trusted memory without validation. Memory is segmented by user and session. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-009, fields 9, 10, 12 and 13, and TP-AGT-009. Before each run, the runtime checks the prompt, template and scaffold against the approved digest. A mismatch stops the run. Stricter bar. Configuration inspection meets the new pass indicator.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-010, fields 9, 10, 12 and 13, and TP-AGT-010. At each stage of the approved process map, the agent calls only the tools that the map allows. Stricter bar. No recognised test method covers this control.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-011, fields 2, 5, 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-AGT-011. The agent's identity or role cannot write, change or delete its own trace store. The same applies to its monitor and its gate configuration. No consequential step executes while the monitor is stopped. This limb is marked Emerging Practice. Stricter bar. Fields 2 and 5 say that the named gates and limits act without the trace. The evidence that they held depends on the trace.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-002, fields 9 and 12. Where the assistant can act, each irreversible or high-materiality action passes the IRQ-AGT-012 gate. The monitor of long sessions is marked as the backstop. No bar is stricter.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-005, field 12, and TP-SEC-005. A planted passage must be withheld from the model context before assembly. A flag that lets the passage reach the model does not pass. Stricter bar.</li>\n<li>IRQ-SEC-008, fields 9, 10, 12, 13 and 14, and TP-SEC-008. An agent's outbound channel sends only to approved destinations. DLP must block confidential-category content before transmission. A flag that lets the transmission continue does not pass. Stricter bar.</li>\n<li>IRQ-GOV-002, field 6. The IRQ-SEC-008 egress control is in front of the egress step. Discovery finds what the control lets through.</li>\n<li>CANVASES_HUM.md, the domain preamble. A misused agent cannot pass the hold in front of a consequential step. A reviewer who must recognise the bad act is not that control.</li>\n<li>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md, the CLM-13 rationale. CLM-13 is the evidence claim. CLM-12 is the control in front of each agent action. The claim text does not change.</li>\n<li>VOLUME_TEMPLATES.md, the illustrative safety-case sub-claims. A sixth example says that consequential agent actions are gated before they execute.</li>\n<li>REGISTER_STATS.md, deliberate gap 2b. The AGT gates hold whether or not a deceptive model is detected.</li>\n<li>IRQ-AGT-013, field 9. The structural confirmation gate shows the complete action parameters as the system will execute them, not the model's rationale. This goes with the CLM-15 change below. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li>IRQ_REGISTER.md, and PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §9, the control objectives of IRQ-AGT-007, IRQ-AGT-010 and IRQ-AGT-014. IRQ-AGT-007: each consequential action that rests on another agent's output is checked outside the agents. IRQ-AGT-010: actual agent trajectories cannot release an outcome that diverges from the approved process design. Reconciliation against that design is evidence. IRQ-AGT-014: each egress and publication action is gated. Real-time monitoring is the backstop.</li>\n<li>CLAIMS_REGISTER.md, the claim text of CLM-11, CLM-14, CLM-15 and CLM-21. CLM-11: components are also pinned and verified before load. CLM-14: delegation is gated under CLM-12 wherever one agent's output drives a consequential action. Reconciliation to the designed process is evidence. CLM-15: approvals show the complete action parameters as the system will execute them and a plain risk summary, not the model's rationale. Approvals bind to those parameters. CLM-21: no third party can alter production behaviour without the firm detecting the change and re-approving it before it relies on the changed behaviour. Versions are pinned where the provider allows it. Where it does not, the firm detects the change and re-approves it before relying on the changed behaviour. The claim titles and criticality ratings do not change. An earlier opinion rests on the earlier claim text.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"5. AI agents that customers and third parties operate: limbs in IRQ-AGT-012 and IRQ-AGT-011","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>The customer's mandate as the clearance input, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_012.md</code> fields 9, 10, 12, 13, 18 and 20, and TP-AGT-012.</strong> Some AI agents act for a customer or a third party. Such an agent can act on the firm's own AI systems. There, the gate clears a consequential action only against the customer's mandate. Before the gate clears the action, code outside the model checks the mandate. It checks the scope, amount, payee, expiry and revocation. An unknown constraint fails the check. The agent's statement of its authority does not clear the action. A valid agent signature alone does not clear it. The procedure adds outside-agent cases to its test design, a step, a measure and an evidence output. Their effort is not yet estimated. Field 18 states the limit of the evidence. The sources describe the control. No published method tests it. Stricter bar where an outside agent's requests can reach the gate.</li>\n<li><strong>The outside agent and its mandate in the trace, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_011.md</code> fields 9, 10, 12, 13 and 20, and TP-AGT-011.</strong> For each action of such an agent, the trace records the agent's identifier, the customer, the mandate reference, the action and the decision. It also records each revocation of the mandate and its cause. The procedure adds an outside-agent run to its test design, a step and a measure. The effort of the step is not yet estimated. Stricter bar where an outside agent's requests reach the firm's AI systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Scope of the two limbs.</strong> The limbs apply where an outside agent's requests reach the firm's own AI systems; <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/TAXONOMY.md</code> line 5 puts such agents in scope. The resistance of the firm's login, authentication and payment-authorisation controls, other than its own AI systems, to an agent stays outside the standard. A request with no agent signal can come as if from the customer. The firm's AI system cannot tell it from the customer's own, so it is not graded. Both limbs are marked Emerging Practice. The limbs add no IRQ. They change no star rating, claim, register question, risk-profile value or reviewer-day figure.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"6. Incident evidence and registers","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>A route from detected misuse to a decision, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_SEC_012.md</code> field 9.</strong> Field 9 adds a control, marked Emerging Practice. Where the firm's monitoring detects a customer using the system against a third party, a defined route takes the case to a named owner. The owner decides by stated criteria whether to act or to report. The source is one AI provider's statements of its own practice, of 26 August 2025 and 28 April 2026. They are about threats of violence to persons. They are not financial-services guidance. No published method tests the route. The ★1 rating does not change. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Installed agent tools, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_AGT_003.md</code> field 9.</strong> Field 9 adds a control, marked Emerging Practice. Where staff machines carry an agent command-line tool, a managed setting turns off the mode in which the tool acts without asking for approval. Two publishers document such a setting. Their reference pages were read on 17 November 2026. The Nx package compromise of August 2025 is the reason. Its script tried to use local AI tools. The bullet states its limits. Neither publisher states the setting as a defence against a local process that starts the tool. One publisher's settings path can be changed by an environment variable. Neither Nx document says in which mode the script started the tools. The ★2 rating does not change. No pass or fail indicator changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>Row 102, <code>V1_Research_Compendium/INCIDENT_REGISTER.md</code>, <code>V2_IRQ_Battery/canvases/CANVASES_MOD_016.md</code> field 10, and <code>README.md</code>.</strong> Row 102 is added. A UK public body rolled out an AI tool that summarises asylum interviews. An advocacy group reports the body's FOI data. Specialists failed 45 of 203 sampled live summaries at the first check (22%). The group estimates that 0.3% of the summaries were checked. The row carries the secondary form, because the FOI response itself was not read. It does not use the paper's second-stage figures. The paper reports 30 failures in 56 second-stage reviews, and 15% of sampled summaries as not fit for use, but 56 does not match the 19 summaries flagged at the first stage. It does not compare the result with the pilot figure. A new note in the register records the row. IRQ-MOD-016 cites the row in field 10 as a worked example of a post-deployment check. The heading of those worked examples now names the incident register. Its parenthesis now says that the first-party disclosures illustrate the L3 measurement and that the register rows are instances of a result in deployment. The README count of incidents moves from 101 to 102. No star rating or risk-profile value changes in this entry.</li>\n<li><strong>The OWASP Top 10 for Agentic Applications row, <code>V5_Regulatory_Crosswalk/SOURCE_REGISTER_REGULATORY.md</code> §12.</strong> The row said that FAIAS cites no ASI identifier and that the publication had not been read. This version cites ASI06 in IRQ-AGT-008 field 9 and ASI10 in IRQ-AGT-010 field 9, from the publication, read on 17 November 2026. A dated note on the row now says this, and the row no longer says that FAIAS cites no ASI identifier. No star rating, claim, question, axis value or reviewer-day figure changes in this entry.</li>\n</ul>"},{"h":"7. Release lines, `README.md`","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>The version line, the banner and the errata record, <code>README.md</code> lines 3 and 121.</strong> The version line names this release, 1.3.6, in place of 1.3.5. The banner gives its date, 22 November 2026, in place of 27 November 2026. The errata record names this correction log, <code>00_Admin/CORRECTION_LOG_2026-11-22.md</code>, as \"v1.3.6, changes to text signed at v1.3.5\".</li>\n</ul>"}]};
